<h1>GitHub Action</h1>
<p class="subtitle">The default reports findings without blocking. Pin and configure the scanner behavior you actually want.</p>

<h2 id="minimal">Conservative blocking example</h2>
<div class="code-block"><span class="kw">name</span>: Gate
<span class="kw">on</span>:
  <span class="kw">pull_request</span>:
  <span class="kw">push</span>:

<span class="kw">permissions</span>:
  <span class="kw">contents</span>: read
  <span class="kw">pull-requests</span>: write
  <span class="kw">security-events</span>: write

<span class="kw">jobs</span>:
  <span class="kw">scan</span>:
    <span class="kw">runs-on</span>: ubuntu-latest
    <span class="kw">steps</span>:
      - <span class="kw">uses</span>: actions/checkout@v4
      - <span class="kw">name</span>: Pin the Gate CLI used at runtime
        <span class="kw">run</span>: npm install -g @penumbraforge/gate@{{ versions.gate.version }}
      - <span class="kw">uses</span>: penumbraforge/gate@v2
        <span class="kw">with</span>:
          <span class="kw">mode</span>: enforce
          <span class="kw">fail-on</span>: high
          <span class="kw">verify</span>: <span class="val">false</span>
          <span class="kw">format</span>: sarif</div>

<p>The action scans <code>gate scan --all</code>, so scope is the checked-out files known to Git. Use a full checkout if other workflow logic depends on history. Treat a scan as one control, not repository proof.</p>

<h2 id="pinning">The action tag does not pin the npm CLI by itself</h2>
<p>Unless <code>use-local: true</code> is set, the bundled action first uses any <code>gate</code> already on <code>PATH</code>. If none is found, it runs <code>npm install -g @penumbraforge/gate</code> without a version and uses the then-current npm release. The <code>penumbraforge/gate@v2</code> ref pins action code, not necessarily scanner code.</p>
<p>For reproducibility, install an explicit npm version before the action as shown above and retain the workflow log. A moving major tag is also not immutable; pin a reviewed commit SHA when your threat model requires it.</p>

<div class="warning-box"><strong><code>use-local: true</code> is for self-testing.</strong> It runs <code>node $GITHUB_WORKSPACE/bin/gate.js</code> from the repository being scanned. In an ordinary pull-request workflow that means executing checked-out repository code. Do not enable it on untrusted contributions.</div>

<h2 id="inputs">Inputs and defaults</h2>
<table class="cmd-table">
  <thead><tr><th>Input</th><th>Default</th><th>Current behavior</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>mode</code></td><td><code>report</code></td><td><code>report</code> warns on threshold findings; <code>enforce</code> fails the step.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>failure-mode</code></td><td>derived from mode</td><td>Explicit <code>block</code> or <code>warn</code> overrides mode for findings and scan errors.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>fail-on</code></td><td><code>high</code></td><td>Keeps critical/high by default. Valid intended values: critical, high, medium, low.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>verify</code></td><td><code>false</code></td><td>Adds <code>--verify</code>; matched credentials may be sent to provider endpoints and severity can change.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>format</code></td><td><code>text</code></td><td>The primary scan is always captured as JSON. Only <code>sarif</code> adds a second SARIF scan and upload attempt.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>github-token</code></td><td><code>github.token</code></td><td>Used for PR comments and SARIF upload.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>slack-webhook</code></td><td>empty</td><td>Sends an optional finding summary to that webhook.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>use-local</code></td><td><code>false</code></td><td>Runs the checked-out repository's <code>bin/gate.js</code>.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="failure">Blocking semantics</h2>
<table class="cmd-table">
  <thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Report/warn</th><th>Enforce/block</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Finding at or above threshold</td><td>Warning; step can succeed.</td><td><code>core.setFailed</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Scan reports unreadable files/errors</td><td>Warning and early return.</td><td><code>core.setFailed</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Install, spawn, or JSON parse failure</td><td colspan="2">Action-level exception fails the step.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>SARIF upload, PR comment, or Slack failure</td><td colspan="2">Warning; the scan result continues.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>The threshold is applied after Gate verification changes severity. Because an <code>inactive</code> verification currently downgrades a finding to low, enabling verification can remove it from the default high threshold.</p>

<h2 id="outputs">Outputs</h2>
<table class="cmd-table">
  <thead><tr><th>Output</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>findings-count</code></td><td>Findings at or above <code>fail-on</code>, not the total detected count.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>blocked</code></td><td>Whether threshold findings exist and the resolved failure mode is block. Scan-error blocking is not represented by this expression.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>scan-report</code></td><td>JSON with threshold findings, errors, skipped entries, summary, and timestamp.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><code>scan-report</code> is capped below GitHub's 1 MiB step-output limit. When necessary Gate keeps the most severe prefix and adds <code>truncated: true</code> plus <code>totalFindings</code>.</p>

<h2 id="comments">PR comments</h2>
<p>On pull-request events with threshold findings, Gate attempts to delete earlier issue comments containing its marker and create a new table with up to 25 file paths, lines, rule names, and severities. It does not intentionally include the secret value. Fork permissions and token policy can prevent this; failure is only a warning.</p>

<h2 id="sarif">SARIF</h2>
<p>With <code>format: sarif</code>, the action runs the repository scan again, base64-encodes the SARIF, and calls GitHub's code-scanning upload API. This creates extra scan time and can produce a result that differs if files or external verification state change between runs. Upload requires suitable <code>security-events: write</code> permission and repository support.</p>

<h2 id="slack">Slack and other network routes</h2>
<p>If <code>slack-webhook</code> is configured and threshold findings exist, Gate sends repository/event metadata, actor, run URL, count, and up to five file/rule labels to Slack. <code>verify: true</code> separately sends credentials to provider endpoints. npm installation, GitHub API calls, checkout, and SARIF upload are additional network routes.</p>

<h2 id="review">Practical CI policy</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Pin the action and the CLI separately.</li>
  <li>Start with verification disabled and an explicit block threshold.</li>
  <li>Protect workflow and ignore/config files with code review.</li>
  <li>Fail independently on scanner execution errors.</li>
  <li>Use a second secret scanner and provider-side controls.</li>
  <li>Review the logs to confirm comments/SARIF actually uploaded.</li>
</ol>
